Show snouty's settings, and trust or deny a project settings file

`settings show` prints the settings that snouty resolved, and the settings files
it read. `settings trust` and `settings deny` decide whether snouty uses a
project settings file (./.snouty.toml).

snouty reads two settings files. The global settings file holds your own
settings, and `snouty login` writes it. It is settings.toml in the global
settings directory: $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/snouty, or ~/.config/snouty when
XDG_CONFIG_HOME is not set. The project settings file holds the settings of one
project. It is .snouty.toml in the current directory, and snouty does not look
for it in parent directories. A value in the project settings file overrides the
same value in the global settings file.

To use another project settings file in place of ./.snouty.toml, name it with
--settings or SNOUTY_SETTINGS_PATH. A file that you name must exist, and snouty
uses it without asking you to trust it.

A settings file is TOML. Each setting is a top-level key, or a key in a named
profile, a [profile.NAME] table. Each setting also has an environment variable:

  tenant                   ANTITHESIS_TENANT
  base_url                 ANTITHESIS_BASE_URL
  repository               ANTITHESIS_REPOSITORY
  https_proxy              ANTITHESIS_HTTPS_PROXY
  container_engine         SNOUTY_CONTAINER_ENGINE
  update_channel           SNOUTY_UPDATE_CHANNEL
  api_cache_max_file_size  SNOUTY_API_CACHE_MAX_FILE_SIZE

Each value is a TOML string, except that api_cache_max_file_size can also be an
integer. tenant is the name of your Antithesis tenant, a hostname such as acme.
base_url is the API URL, by default https://TENANT.antithesis.com. repository is
the container registry that snouty pushes your images to. https_proxy is the URL
of a proxy for snouty's requests. container_engine is docker or podman; by
default, snouty uses the engine that it finds, podman first. update_channel is
stable, the default, or unstable. api_cache_max_file_size is a size such as "10
MiB", the default, or an integer number of bytes. snouty ignores a key that it
does not know. For example:

  tenant = "acme"
  repository = "registry.example.com/acme/images"

  [profile.staging]
  tenant = "acme-staging"

Select a profile with --profile or ANTITHESIS_PROFILE. For each setting, snouty
uses the first value that it finds: the environment variable, then the selected
profile in the project settings file, the selected profile in the global
settings file, the top-level value in the project settings file, then the
top-level value in the global settings file.

Credentials are not settings, and a settings file must not hold them.
`snouty login` stores them in the system keychain on macOS, and on other systems
in credentials.toml, in the global settings directory. snouty reads
credentials.toml on every system. The file holds a [default] table for the top
level, and a [profile.NAME] table for each profile. Each table has a `type` key,
which selects the other keys:

  type = "ApiKey"    api_key
  type = "OAuth"     antithesis_token, and an optional refresh_token
  type = "Password"  username and password (deprecated)

For example:

  [default]
  type = "ApiKey"
  api_key = "<your API key>"

  [profile.staging]
  type = "ApiKey"
  api_key = "<your API key>"

For each command, snouty uses the first credential that it finds:
ANTITHESIS_API_KEY, or ANTITHESIS_USERNAME with ANTITHESIS_PASSWORD, then the
keychain entry and the credentials.toml table of the selected profile, then
those of [default], then a GitHub Actions OIDC token. Keep credentials.toml
readable only by you (mode 0600).

snouty can rewrite settings.toml and credentials.toml, for example in
`snouty login`. A rewrite keeps the values of the other profiles. It removes
comments, can change the order of keys, and in credentials.toml removes keys
that snouty does not know. When the file does not parse, snouty renames it to
NAME.bak and writes a new file that holds only the new values.

A project settings file can set any setting, at the top level or in a profile,
including base_url, https_proxy and repository. snouty sends your API key to
base_url, sends its requests through https_proxy, and pushes your images to
repository, so a settings file from a cloned repository could send your
credentials or images to another server. Read the whole file, with every profile
in it, before you trust it. If you do not want snouty to use the file, run
`snouty settings deny`.

Examples:
  snouty settings show
  snouty settings trust
  snouty settings deny

Usage: snouty settings [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  show   Print the settings that snouty resolved
  trust  Use a project settings file while its content stays the same
  deny   Ignore a project settings file
  help   Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

      --json
          Output JSON where supported (NDJSON for list/stream commands, pretty
          JSON otherwise)

      --verbose
          Log API requests to stderr (authentication tokens redacted)

      --settings <SETTINGS>
          Path to the snouty settings file (default: ./.snouty.toml; overrides
          SNOUTY_SETTINGS_PATH)

      --profile <PROFILE>
          Settings profile to select (overrides ANTITHESIS_PROFILE)
